    {
      "hypothesis": "If the over-merging the reviewer saw in the upper half (regions spanning many parallel laths, real median width 17 px against about 10 px measured lath spacing) comes from a ridge scale and threshold too coarse for the thin bright beta ribs, then a narrower scale with higher sensitivity will cut those regions into single laths. If the scratch false positive near x=1175, y=250-340 is a lath edge, then a larger minimum crossing angle and length will remove it. The test is falsified if the next review still shows multi-lath regions crossing colony boundaries.",
      "rationale": "The twin is untrusted, so no search is run and only the start params are judged, by the next review of the real drawings. I therefore change only five params, each tied to the reviewer's evidence.\n\nChanges:\n- polarity is set to bright_boundaries. The reviewer reads this as a BSE image with thin bright beta ribs; auto probably already chose this, so making it explicit only removes ambiguity.\n- boundary_width_px goes 2.5 -> 2.0. The reviewer asked for a finer ridge scale. I stop short of the suggested 1-1.5 px because the measured boundary width is about 4 px.\n- boundary_sensitivity goes 1.0 -> 0.8, the reviewer's suggested 0.8x threshold, to find fainter ribs.\n- scratch_min_crossing_deg goes 12 -> 20, matching the reviewer's suggested 20 deg minimum angle to the local flakes.\n- scratch_min_length_px goes 200 -> 500, about 0.3 of the 1728 px image width as suggested. No real scratch is visible on this image, so a stricter detector loses little.\n\nWhat I left alone:\n- Gap bridging is already on (9 px), so the reviewer's bridging suggestion is already in place.\n- I did not raise min_area_px, to keep the change set small and attributable.\n\nConfidence that this fixes the real drawing is low, about 30%. The reviewer warns that the ribs may be at the resolution limit, in which case no parameter helps. The search space below is listed for completeness; it is not run on this untrusted twin.",
